## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a practical, testable fair-use layer for a multi-tenant LLM gateway with billing considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas and burst handling using a token bucket
- Isolation of prompts and responses per tenant
- Simple REST API for quota status and consumption
- Observability and a minimal test plan for bucket behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillPerMs) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillPerMs = refillPerMs;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  tick() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - this.lastRefill;
    const refill = Math.floor(elapsed * this.refillPerMs);
    if (refill > 0) {
      this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + refill);
      this.lastRefill = now;
    }
  }
  tryConsume(n = 1) {
    this.tick();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests designing a simple, resilient per-tenant model routing with clear failover, suitable for beginners while demonstrating practical data modeling, API surface, and basic testing.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant config: pinnedModelVersion, fallbackVersion
- Health checks and routing logic
- Failover handling and latency observability
- Simple durable storage for configs

## Code Example

```javascript
function chooseModelVersion(tenantId, healthMap, configStore) {
  const cfg = configStore.get(tenantId);
  const ok = healthMap[cfg?.pinnedModelVersion];
  if (cfg && ok) return cfg.pinnedModelVersion;
  return cfg?.fallbackVersion;
}
```

